## The idea

Put the weights end to end on a line. Index i owns a piece as long as w[i]. Pick a random point on the line and find whose piece it lands in: the first **running total** greater than the point. Running totals are sorted, so use **binary search**.

## Step by step

1. Build running totals (long, to avoid overflow).
2. r = random number in [0, total).
3. Binary search for the first total > r.
4. Return that index.

## Worked example

**Input:** w = [1, 3]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| totals | [1, 4] |
| r = 0 | first total > 0 is 1 → index 0 (1 chance in 4) |
| r = 1, 2 or 3 | first total > r is 4 → index 1 (3 chances in 4) |

**Result:** index 1 about 75% of the time

## Cost

O(n) setup, O(log n) per pick.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 528 — pick index i with probability w[i] / (sum of w).
public sealed class WeightedPicker {
    private readonly long[] _prefix;       // _prefix[i] = w[0] + ... + w[i]; long, so big weights cannot overflow
    private readonly Random _rnd;

    public WeightedPicker(int[] w) : this(w, new Random()) { }
    public WeightedPicker(int[] w, Random rnd) {
        if (w == null || w.Length == 0) throw new ArgumentException("no weights");
        _prefix = new long[w.Length];
        long sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < w.Length; i++) {
            if (w[i] < 0) throw new ArgumentException("negative weight");
            sum += w[i];
            _prefix[i] = sum;
        }
        if (sum == 0) throw new ArgumentException("all weights are zero");
        _rnd = rnd;
    }

    public int PickIndex() {
        long r = _rnd.NextInt64(_prefix[_prefix.Length - 1]);   // a random point in [0, total)
        // The first index whose running total is greater than r owns that point. Binary search: O(log n).
        int lo = 0, hi = _prefix.Length - 1;
        while (lo < hi) {
            int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
            if (_prefix[mid] > r) hi = mid; else lo = mid + 1;
        }
        return lo;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Off-by-one?**

Point r in [0, total); the owner is the first prefix > r. With [1, total] you need ≥ instead — pick one and test the boundaries.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n) setup, O(log n) per pick.

**Q: O(1) per pick?**

The alias method (Vose): O(n) setup, two random numbers per pick.

**Q: Many threads pick.**

The totals are read-only; only Random needs care — Random.Shared.

**Q: Weights change often.**

A Fenwick tree over the weights: update and pick both O(log n).

**Q: Where is this used?**

Load balancing by capacity, ad selection, A/B test traffic splits.
